          Fertilize a cool-season lawn (Kentucky bluegrass, tall fescue, ryegrass) mainly in early fall, around September, with a second feeding in late fall and at most a light one in late spring. Fertilize a warm-season lawn (Bermuda, zoysia, St. Augustine, centipede) after it has fully greened up in late spring, then every six to eight weeks until about six weeks before your first frost. Everything else on this page is about the details that move those dates: soil temperature, how fast your fertilizer releases, whether the lawn is new, and the handful of times you should not feed at all.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Your Grass Type Sets the Calendar
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grass is fed when it can use the nitrogen, and that happens when it is actively growing. Cool-season grasses grow hardest in the mild weather of spring and fall and slow down in summer heat. Warm-season grasses go dormant and brown in winter, wake up as the soil warms, and grow fastest in the heat of mid-summer. Feed each type during its own growing window and the fertilizer turns into roots and leaf. Feed it outside that window and you mostly feed weeds, disease or the storm drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why one national schedule printed on a fertilizer bag cannot be right for every yard. A four-step program that starts in early spring was designed around northern cool-season lawns, and even there most extension services now push the emphasis toward fall. Apply the same program to Bermuda in Georgia and the first step lands while the grass is still half dormant, which is exactly when you want to leave it alone.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grass type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main feeding</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Secondary feeding</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stop by</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cool-season (bluegrass, tall fescue, ryegrass, fine fescue)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Early fall, around September</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late fall, plus an optional light late-spring feed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before the ground freezes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Warm-season (Bermuda, zoysia, St. Augustine)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After full green-up, late April to May</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 6-8 weeks through summer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6 weeks before first frost</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Centipede and bahia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After full green-up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One more light feed in summer, if any</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Late summer</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you want a single rule to remember, it is this: cool-season lawns get the majority of their annual nitrogen between late August and November, and warm-season lawns get the majority between late spring and late summer. The rest of this guide explains how to narrow that down to the right weekend for your yard, and how to avoid the timing mistakes that undo a good product. Our <a href="/best-fertilizer-for-grass/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best lawn fertilizer picks</a> cover which formulas suit which feeding.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest caveat before the details. Fertilizer timing matters, but it ranks below mowing height, watering and soil condition. A lawn mowed too short on compacted clay will not be rescued by perfectly timed feedings. If your grass is thin and the soil is hard enough to resist a screwdriver, sort out compaction first; our guide on <a href="/when-to-aerate-lawn/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">when to aerate your lawn</a> pairs naturally with the fall feeding described below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grass-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Your Grass Cool-Season or Warm-Season?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most homeowners know their grass only as green. The quickest way to tell the two families apart is to watch the lawn through a year. If it stays green through a mild winter, looks its best in May and October, and struggles in July and August, it is almost certainly a cool-season grass. If it turns tan after the first hard frost, stays brown until spring, and looks best in the heat of summer, it is a warm-season grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Geography gives a strong hint. Lawns across the northern half of the United States, the Pacific Northwest and higher elevations are dominated by Kentucky bluegrass, tall fescue, perennial ryegrass and fine fescues. Lawns across the South, the Gulf Coast, Florida and the desert Southwest are mostly Bermuda, zoysia, St. Augustine, centipede and bahia. The band in between, from roughly Virginia and Kentucky west through Oklahoma, is called the transition zone, and both types grow there, often in the same neighborhood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Growth habit is the other clue. Bermuda, zoysia and St. Augustine spread by above-ground runners called stolons that you can see creeping over sidewalks and into beds. Kentucky bluegrass spreads by underground rhizomes, so it fills in without obvious runners. Tall fescue and ryegrass are bunch grasses that mostly thicken in place, which is why they need overseeding to fill bare spots. If you pull up a plug and see long, jointed runners on the surface, you are looking at a warm-season grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixed lawns are common in the transition zone, typically tall fescue with patches of invading Bermuda or zoysia. In that case, fertilize for the grass you want to keep. Feeding a fescue lawn in midsummer helps the Bermuda patches more than the fescue, while feeding in fall favors the fescue just as the Bermuda is shutting down. If you cannot identify your grass at all, your county extension office will usually identify a sample for free or a small fee.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="soil-temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Soil Temperature Should You Wait For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Calendar dates are a proxy. What grass actually responds to is soil temperature, and that can vary by three or four weeks from one spring to the next. Cool-season grass roots begin growing when the soil reaches roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit, and top growth picks up as the soil climbs into the mid-50s. Warm-season grasses do not grow meaningfully until the soil holds around 65 degrees, which in most of the South lines up with the point when nights stay above 60.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measuring is easy and cheap. A basic soil thermometer pushed four inches into the ground, read in the morning when soil is coolest, tells you more than any calendar. Take the reading over several days rather than once after a warm afternoon. Many state extension services also publish free soil temperature maps updated daily, which are a good substitute if you do not want to buy a thermometer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE NUMBERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">50-55 degrees for cool-season, 65 degrees for warm-season</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Cool-season grass starts growing as soil passes about 50 to 55 degrees at a four-inch depth. Warm-season grass waits for a steady 65. Fertilizer applied below those numbers mostly sits there, and quick-release nitrogen can leach or run off before roots take it up.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Soil temperature also governs crabgrass, which matters if your spring fertilizer is bundled with a pre-emergent herbicide. Crabgrass starts germinating once soil holds around 55 degrees for several consecutive days. A pre-emergent needs to be down before that, which is why the old advice to apply it when forsythia blooms works reasonably well. The trap is that the crabgrass window usually comes before cool-season grass needs much nitrogen, so a combination product can push you to fertilize earlier than the grass wants.
          </p>

          <p>
            In fall the logic runs in reverse. Cool-season grass keeps growing roots until the soil drops to around 40 degrees, well after top growth has slowed and air temperatures feel wintry. That gap between leaf growth stopping and root growth stopping is the window for the late-fall feeding described next. Warm-season grass, by contrast, begins slowing as soil falls below the mid-60s, and that is your cue to stop feeding it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fall-feeding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Is the Fall Feeding the One That Matters Most?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For cool-season lawns, fall is when fertilizer does the most good and the least harm. The grass is recovering from summer stress, temperatures favor root growth over leaf growth, and weeds are winding down rather than starting up. Nitrogen applied in early fall thickens the turf by encouraging new shoots, called tillers, and helps bare or thin areas fill in before winter. Most university turf programs recommend putting half or more of a cool-season lawn's yearly nitrogen down between September and November.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical fall plan uses two applications. The first goes down in early fall, roughly late August in the far north to late September in the transition zone, as the summer heat breaks. The second, often called the late-fall or winterizer feeding, goes down when top growth has nearly stopped but the grass is still green, which for many lawns is around the time of the last or second-to-last mowing. That timing lets the plant store carbohydrates in its roots instead of pushing soft leaf growth into a frost.
          </p>

          <p>
            The payoff shows up the following spring. Lawns fed well in fall green up earlier, need less nitrogen in spring, and enter summer with deeper roots, which makes them more drought tolerant. That is why extension services increasingly tell homeowners that if they only fertilize once a year, they should do it in early fall, not in spring. A dense fall lawn also crowds out next year's weeds better than any spring weed and feed product will.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not confuse winterizer marketing with a special chemistry. A late-fall fertilizer is mainly about timing and a moderate dose of nitrogen; the label name adds nothing a standard lawn fertilizer would not do if applied on the same day. Do not feed warm-season grass in late fall at all. Nitrogen applied as Bermuda or St. Augustine heads toward dormancy encourages tender growth that is more prone to winter injury and disease.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fall is also the natural time to combine feeding with aeration and overseeding on cool-season lawns. Aerate, overseed, then fertilize, and the seed and fertilizer both drop into the cores. If you are seeding, our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-it-take-grass-seed-to-grow/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long grass seed takes to grow</a> explains why early fall gives new seedlings the best odds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spring-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Goes Wrong When You Fertilize in Spring?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spring is when lawn care aisles fill up and when most people feel the urge to do something. It is also when fertilizer is most often wasted. The most common mistake is feeding a cool-season lawn heavily in March or early April. The grass is already growing on stored reserves, and a big dose of quick-release nitrogen produces a flush of soft leaf growth at the expense of roots, which is the opposite of what the lawn needs heading into summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            That spring surge has knock-on costs. You mow more often, sometimes twice a week. The lush, nitrogen-rich blades are more susceptible to fungal diseases such as brown patch once heat and humidity arrive. And because the roots were short-changed, the lawn wilts faster in the first dry spell. A light feeding in late spring, around May, using a slow-release product, is the better choice for a cool-season lawn that was fed properly the previous fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm-season lawns have the opposite problem. Their owners often fertilize at the first sign of green, when the grass is only partly out of dormancy. Early nitrogen feeds the cool-season weeds that are actively growing at that point, such as annual bluegrass and henbit, and in Bermuda lawns it has been associated with more spring dead spot. Wait until the lawn is fully green and has been mowed at least once or twice, which usually means late April to May.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weed and feed products deserve their own warning. A granular broadleaf weed and feed needs to stick to wet weed leaves to work, so it is best applied to a dew-covered lawn when broadleaf weeds are actively growing, and it should not be watered in for a day or two. That timing often does not line up with the ideal time to fertilize. Separate products, timed separately, usually work better; our <a href="/best-weed-killer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">weed killer guide</a> covers spot treatments that avoid blanket herbicide use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not fertilize a lawn that is dormant from drought. Brown, crunchy cool-season grass in July is protecting itself. Nitrogen will not green it up without water, and the salts in some fertilizers can add stress. Wait for rain or irrigation to bring it back, then wait for the fall feeding window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Often Should You Fertilize, and How Much?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most lawns need two to four feedings a year. Cool-season lawns typically do well with two fall applications plus one light spring feed. Warm-season lawns generally get fed every six to eight weeks through their growing season, which works out to three or four applications for Bermuda and fewer for low-input grasses. Slow-release products stretch the interval to eight to ten weeks, while quick-release products need shorter intervals and smaller doses.
          </p>

          <p>
            The way turf professionals measure dose is pounds of actual nitrogen per 1,000 square feet. A common ceiling for a single application is one pound of nitrogen per 1,000 square feet, and closer to half a pound if most of the nitrogen is quick-release. Yearly totals vary by grass, as the table shows. Bermuda is the hungriest home lawn grass; centipede is famously low-input and can decline if overfed.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Grass</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical yearly nitrogen (lb per 1,000 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kentucky bluegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher end for irrigated, high-traffic lawns</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tall fescue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most of it in fall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Perennial ryegrass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Prone to disease if overfed in summer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine fescue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low-input; thrives on neglect in shade</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bermuda</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fed through summer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Zoysia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Overfeeding builds thatch</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">St. Augustine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Avoid feeding in high heat stress</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Centipede</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low-input; overfeeding causes decline</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Converting that to bag weight is simple arithmetic. The first number on a fertilizer bag is its nitrogen percentage. A 24-0-6 product is 24 percent nitrogen, so delivering one pound of nitrogen takes about 4.2 pounds of product per 1,000 square feet. A 5,000-square-foot lawn would need about 21 pounds for that feeding. Most bags list a spreader setting that already hits a sensible rate, but knowing the math helps you spot a product that is underdosed or a setting that would overdo it. A calibrated <a href="/best-broadcast-spreader/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">broadcast spreader</a> makes those rates repeatable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two habits reduce how much you need to buy. Leaving clippings on the lawn returns nitrogen to the soil, and extension services commonly estimate it can supply up to about a quarter of a lawn's yearly needs; a <a href="/best-mulching-mower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">mulching mower</a> makes that easy. A soil test every three to four years, available through most state extension labs for a modest fee, tells you whether you need phosphorus, potassium or lime at all, so you stop paying for nutrients the soil already has.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-lawns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Should You Fertilize a New Lawn or New Sod?
          </h2>

          <p>
            New lawns follow different rules because seedlings have no root reserves to draw on. When seeding, apply a starter fertilizer at the same time you sow, or just before, and rake it lightly into the top inch of soil. Starter fertilizers carry more phosphorus than maintenance blends because phosphorus supports early root development. Several states restrict phosphorus in lawn fertilizer but make an exception for establishing new turf or for soils a test shows are deficient, so check your local rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second feeding comes once the seedlings are established, generally about six to eight weeks after seeding, or after the new grass has been mowed two or three times. Use a light rate, around half a pound of nitrogen per 1,000 square feet, rather than a full dose. For a cool-season lawn seeded in early fall, that second feeding often lines up neatly with the late-fall application, so the timing almost takes care of itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sod is further along than seed but still has a limited root system. Apply starter fertilizer to the soil before laying sod, or right after installation, then wait about four to six weeks, until the sod has knitted down and resists being lifted at the corners, before feeding again on a normal schedule. Watering is far more important than fertilizer during those first weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep weed and feed products and most herbicides away from new grass. Many labels say to wait until a new lawn has been mowed three or four times before applying broadleaf herbicides, and most crabgrass pre-emergents will stop grass seed from germinating, too. If you seeded with a fast-starting variety from our <a href="/best-grass-seed/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">grass seed picks</a>, you may be mowing within three or four weeks, but slower species like Kentucky bluegrass push that schedule out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overseeding an existing lawn sits between the two cases. Use a starter fertilizer at the time of seeding if the soil test supports it, then resume the normal schedule. In practice that means your fall overseeding, your early-fall feeding and your late-fall feeding all fit into the same eight to ten weeks, which is one more reason cool-season lawn work concentrates in autumn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Following the bag's four-step dates blindly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                National programs start in early spring because that is when people shop, not because every lawn needs nitrogen then. Match the steps to your grass type and soil temperature. For a cool-season lawn, shifting weight from spring to fall usually gives a thicker lawn with fewer weeds for the same money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeding warm-season grass at the first green</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A half-dormant Bermuda or St. Augustine lawn cannot use much nitrogen yet, but the winter weeds in it can. Wait until the lawn is fully green and has been mowed once or twice, usually late April to May across the South, then begin the six to eight week schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fertilizing before a heavy storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Light rain after fertilizing helps move granules into the soil. A downpour washes them onto driveways and into storm drains, wasting product and feeding algae downstream. Check the forecast, sweep any granules off hard surfaces back onto the lawn, and water in with about a quarter inch instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeding drought-dormant or heat-stressed grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brown cool-season grass in midsummer is dormant, not hungry. Nitrogen without water does nothing useful and can add salt stress. Water it back to life if you want it green, but hold fertilizer until the early-fall window when temperatures drop and the grass begins actively growing again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the soil test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a test you are guessing about phosphorus, potassium and pH. Many lawns already have plenty of phosphorus, while acidic soil can limit how well grass uses any fertilizer at all. A test every three to four years costs little through an extension lab and often saves more than its price.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to fertilize before or after rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Apply before a light rain or water in afterward with about a quarter inch of irrigation, which moves granules off the leaves and into the root zone. Avoid applying when a heavy storm is forecast in the next day or two, since runoff wastes product. Weed and feed products are the exception: they need dry weather after application to stay on weed leaves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What month should I fertilize my lawn for the last time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cool-season grass, the last feeding usually falls in October or November, while the grass is still green but top growth has nearly stopped, and before the ground freezes. For warm-season grass, stop about six weeks before your average first frost date, which for much of the South means mid-September at the latest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I fertilize my lawn in summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Warm-season lawns, yes; summer is their peak growing season, so feed every six to eight weeks. Cool-season lawns, mostly no. Heavy nitrogen in heat promotes disease and stresses the grass. If a cool-season lawn genuinely needs color in summer, a very light dose of slow-release or organic fertilizer on irrigated turf is the most you should consider.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I mow before or after fertilizing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mow a day or two before fertilizing, then let the lawn have a few days before the next mowing. Granules settle better on a lawn that is not overly long, and waiting to mow afterward gives water time to move the fertilizer into the soil. If you bag clippings, waiting also avoids collecting the granules you just spread.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it too late to fertilize in November?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a cool-season lawn in most of the northern US, November is often fine for the late-fall feeding as long as the grass is still green and the ground is not frozen. Some states and towns ban fertilizer on frozen ground or after set dates, so check local rules. For warm-season lawns, November is too late; they are heading into dormancy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after fertilizing can kids and pets go on the lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For standard granular fertilizer, most labels say people and pets can return once the product has been watered in and the grass has dried. Liquid products are typically safe once dry. Combination products with herbicides or insecticides often carry longer restrictions, so read the specific label, which is the legal instruction for that product, rather than relying on a general rule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does organic fertilizer change the timing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only slightly. Organic fertilizers release nitrogen as soil microbes break them down, and microbes are slow in cold soil. That makes organic products a good fit for early fall and late spring, when soil is warm, and a weaker choice for the late-fall feeding. They are also gentler, so there is less risk of burning grass if timing or rates are off.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

