        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Fertilizer timing comes down to feeding grass when it is growing. Cool-season lawns get their main meals in early and late fall with a light spring snack. Warm-season lawns get fed from full green-up through late summer, then left alone as they head toward dormancy. Use soil temperature, not the calendar, to fine-tune the dates for your yard.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Get the timing right and you will likely use less fertilizer than you do now, mow less in spring, and fight fewer weeds and diseases. Pair the fall feeding with aeration and overseeding where the lawn needs it, test the soil every few years, and leave the clippings on the grass. That is most of what a good lawn program is.
          </p>
        </div>

